Give HarmonyOS its own operating system in the GPU lists.

Four things had to be true before a GPU rule could reach this platform,
and three of them were silently false.

GetOsType() had no OHOS arm and fell through to kOsAny, so every entry
naming an operating system was inert. gpu/config/BUILD.gn told the
generator the target was linux, and the generator drops every entry whose
os is not the one it was told -- so the Android rules were not merely
unmatched, they were never compiled in. And process_json.py ties each
gl_type to the platforms it has seen it on, which stopped the carried-over
rules at the door because they say gles.

That last one changes what an earlier trial meant. Mapping OHOS to Linux
turned on exactly two entries, 283 and 478, and we read that as "only two
Linux rules can match a Mali device". The real reason is that the binary
held nothing but Linux rules. The 46 others are keyed to Intel, NVIDIA,
AMD, Mesa, VMware or VirtualBox and cannot match a phone; every rule
written for the GPUs that are actually in these devices -- Mali, Adreno,
PowerVR -- says android, and was not there to match.

So: kOsOhos, told to the generator, allowed a gles gl_type, and the
Android rules that are about hardware carried over to it. A rule may come
over when what it matches is the GPU, the vendor, the driver or the GL
strings. A rule that also tests the operating system's version may not,
because HarmonyOS numbers its releases on its own scale and the comparison
would still return something. Six stay behind: one in the software list,
five in the driver bug list.

91 entries carried over, numbered from 900, well clear of upstream's. The
last ten are left for Maleoon, HUAWEI's own GPU, which upstream has never
seen and has no rule for at all; entry 990 holds the place with a renderer
pattern that matches nothing, so it does nothing until someone gives it
one.

Ported by script from the two lists as they stand in 154, so the selection
can be re-run when the lists move. Verified through the generator: the
built lists carry 12 and 81 OHOS entries, which is what went in.

Exported against a tree that already has the other patches, not against
pristine Chromium: git diff shows everything anyone has changed, so the
first export carried the main patch's two IS_OHOS arms and the emulator's
rule 190 as well. Applied in order, the duplicate could not find its
context, which is what a build on a second machine said.

diff --git a/gpu/config/BUILD.gn b/gpu/config/BUILD.gn
index 09bedb08fc..eec8528cc0 100644
--- a/gpu/config/BUILD.gn
+++ b/gpu/config/BUILD.gn
@@ -52,6 +52,11 @@ action("process_json") {
     args += [ "chromeos" ]
   } else if (is_fuchsia) {
     args += [ "fuchsia" ]
+  } else if (is_ohos) {
+    # Without this the generator is told linux, and it drops every entry
+    # whose os is not the one it was told -- so the rules written for this
+    # platform never reached the binary at all.
+    args += [ "ohos" ]
   } else {
     args += [ "linux" ]
   }
diff --git a/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.cc b/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.cc
index a7c6208cb0..4d15fc3007 100644
--- a/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.cc
+++ b/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.cc
@@ -837,6 +837,12 @@ GpuControlList::OsType GpuControlList::GetOsType() {
   return kOsAndroid;
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_FUCHSIA)
   return kOsFuchsia;
+#elif BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // Its own, not Linux. Borrowing Linux turns on 48 entries written for
+  // desktop cards and still leaves the phone GPUs unmatched, because those
+  // rules say android. The entries that belong here are carried over and
+  // marked ohos instead.
+  return kOsOhos;
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX) || BUILDFLAG(IS_OPENBSD)
   return kOsLinux;
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_MAC)
diff --git a/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.h b/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.h
index 12a6cfe578..a504ea7ff5 100644
--- a/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.h
+++ b/gpu/config/gpu_control_list.h
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@ class GPU_CONFIG_EXPORT GpuControlList {
     kOsChromeOS,
     kOsAndroid,
     kOsFuchsia,
+    kOsOhos,
     kOsIOS,
     kOsAny
   };
